@lang en
-- These materials contain information that has been derived from information originally made available by the Province of British Columbia at: http://www.bclaws.gov.bc.ca and this information is being used in accordance with the King's Printer Licence – British Columbia available at: https://www.bclaws.gov.bc.ca/standards/Licence.html. They have not, however, been produced in affiliation with, or with the endorsement of, the Province of British Columbia and THESE MATERIALS ARE NOT AN OFFICIAL VERSION.
--
-- RIVAL READINGS, EXPECTED TO FAIL. Every assertion in this file states what a different reading of
-- the Interpretation Act (British Columbia), RSBC 1996, c. 238 would give, and every one of them
-- is MEANT to fail against bc-interpretation-time.l4, which takes the reading the text supports
-- (NOTES, fork register). The file exists so that a reviewer who prefers a rival reading can see
-- the day it gives and the day the module gives, side by side, and so that a change to the module
-- that silently flips one of these readings turns a count in check.sh.
--
-- check.sh counts this file's failures: it must be exactly 16 (the number in `expected_failed`).
-- A cleaner run (fewer failures) means a rival reading has become true of the module.

IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `bc-interpretation-time`

GIVETH A `Position of the reference day`
`ref before the period` MEANS `The reference day is before the period`

GIVETH A `Position of the reference day`
`ref after the period` MEANS `The reference day is after the period`

GIVEN d IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`test calendar: a day is a holiday` d MEANS
    `s 29 a day is a holiday` (GIVEN x YIELD FALSE) d

GIVEN d IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`test calendar: Saturday and Sunday are holidays` d MEANS
    `s 29 a day is a holiday` (GIVEN x YIELD (`Weekday of` x EQUALS 6)) d

GIVEN d IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`test office: open during regular business hours` d MEANS
        (`Weekday of` d AT LEAST 1 AND `Weekday of` d AT MOST 5)
    AND NOT (d EQUALS YMD 2026 4 3)

GIVEN d IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`test office: regularly closed` d MEANS
    `Weekday of` d EQUALS 0 OR `Weekday of` d EQUALS 6

§ `Rival reading 1: "from" counts the reference day (s 25.2(2)(a) says it does not)`

-- Counting the reference day as day 1, 30 days from 1 January end on 30 January. The text counts
-- "from and including the day after the reference day", so the module says 31 January.
#ASSERT `the last day of a period of days counted from a day` (YMD 2026 1 1) 30 EQUALS YMD 2026 1 30
-- ... and a week from 1 January would end on 7 January, not 8 January.
#ASSERT `the last day of a period of weeks counted from a day` (YMD 2026 1 1) 1 EQUALS YMD 2026 1 7
-- ... and the reference day would be inside the period.
#ASSERT `a day falls inside a period counted from a reference day` (YMD 2026 1 1) (YMD 2026 1 1) `ref before the period` (`Period of time` WITH `unit` IS Days, `how many` IS 30, `clear` IS FALSE)

§ `Rival reading 2: a month is 30 days and a year is 365 days (s 29 and ss 25.3, 25.4 say calendar months)`

-- 31 January plus 30 days is 2 March; the text gives the last day of February.
#ASSERT `the last day of a period of months counted after an event` (YMD 2025 1 31) 1 EQUALS YMD 2025 3 2
-- 1 March 2023 plus 365 days is 29 February 2024; the text gives 1 March 2024.
#ASSERT `the last day of a period of years counted after an event` (YMD 2023 3 1) 1 EQUALS YMD 2024 2 29

§ `Rival reading 3: s 29 "month" read alone (from 1 January to 1 February, less one day)`

-- Taken on its own, a month that begins on 1 January ends on 31 January. s 25.3 counts from the
-- day AFTER the reference day, which puts the end of one month from 1 January on 1 February.
#ASSERT `the last day of a period of months counted after an event` (YMD 2026 1 1) 1 EQUALS YMD 2026 1 31

§ `Rival reading 4: "clear" adds nothing`

-- 30 clear days from 1 January end on 1 February (s 25.2(3)(a)), not on 31 January.
#ASSERT `s 25.2 beginning or end of a period of days` (YMD 2026 1 1) `ref before the period` 30 TRUE EQUALS YMD 2026 1 31

§ `Rival reading 5: the holiday rule moves only a period in days`

-- 1 month from 5 March 2026 ends on Sunday 5 April. s 25(3) names ss 25.2, 25.3 and 25.4, so the
-- end moves to Monday 6 April (the test calendar below has no other holiday).
#ASSERT (`the span of days of a period for doing an act` `test calendar: a day is a holiday` (YMD 2026 3 5) `ref before the period` (`Period of time` WITH `unit` IS Months, `how many` IS 1, `clear` IS FALSE))'s `last day` EQUALS YMD 2026 4 5
#ASSERT (`the span of days of a period for doing an act` `test calendar: a day is a holiday` (YMD 2025 4 5) `ref before the period` (`Period of time` WITH `unit` IS Years, `how many` IS 1, `clear` IS FALSE))'s `last day` EQUALS YMD 2026 4 5

§ `Rival reading 6: Saturday is a holiday (the text lists Sunday, not Saturday)`

-- 30 days from 1 January 2026 end on Saturday 31 January. If Saturday were a holiday the end would
-- move past Sunday to Monday 2 February.
#ASSERT (`the span of days of a period for doing an act` `test calendar: Saturday and Sunday are holidays` (YMD 2026 1 1) `ref before the period` (`Period of time` WITH `unit` IS Days, `how many` IS 30, `clear` IS FALSE))'s `last day` EQUALS YMD 2026 1 31

§ `Rival reading 7: a closed office is treated like a holiday (s 25(4)(b) needs two conditions)`

-- The office is shut on Friday 3 April 2026 for a reason that is not its regular closing. With the
-- reference day after the period, s 25(3)(b) would look back (to Thursday 2 April); s 25(4) looks
-- forward, to Monday 6 April, because limb (b) needs the office to be REGULARLY closed that day.
#ASSERT `s 25(4) the beginning or end of a period for doing an act in a business office, moved to a day the office is open` `test office: open during regular business hours` `test office: regularly closed` `ref after the period` (YMD 2026 4 3) EQUALS YMD 2026 4 2

§ `Rival reading 8: Pacific Time follows daylight saving time (s 26(1) fixes 7 hours)`

-- A reading that took Pacific Time from the wall clock instead of from s 26(1) would put the
-- offset at 8 hours in winter, and midnight at 08:00 UTC (the figure 8 is not from the text).
-- The text says "7 hours behind", without exception, and does not mention daylight saving time.
#ASSERT (`s 26 the UTC moment of a Pacific Time moment` (`s 4(1) the beginning of a day` (YMD 2026 1 15)))'s `hour of the day` EQUALS 8

§ `Rival reading 9: a person reaches an age at the end of the birthday (s 25.1 says the beginning)`

#ASSERT NOT (`s 25.1 a person has reached an age on a day` (YMD 2000 5 17) 18 (YMD 2018 5 17))

§ `Rival reading 10: a period for doing an act that ends in a closed office ignores a regular closing`

-- A Sunday end, reference day after the period, an office regularly closed on Sunday: s 25(4)(b)
-- looks back to Friday 10 April. A reading that always looks forward gives Monday 13 April.
#ASSERT `s 25(4) the beginning or end of a period for doing an act in a business office, moved to a day the office is open` `test office: open during regular business hours` `test office: regularly closed` `ref after the period` (YMD 2026 4 12) EQUALS YMD 2026 4 13

§ `Rival reading 11: s 25(3) moves only the far end of a period for doing an act`

-- On this reading a holiday on the day next to the reference day does not move: the period "begins"
-- where counting starts. The module reads "begins or ends" on its words and moves both ends.
-- One day from Saturday 4 April 2026: counting starts on Sunday 5 April, a holiday.
#ASSERT (`the span of days of a period for doing an act` `test calendar: a day is a holiday` (YMD 2026 4 4) `ref before the period` (`Period of time` WITH `unit` IS Days, `how many` IS 1, `clear` IS FALSE))'s `first day` EQUALS YMD 2026 4 5
#ASSERT `a day falls inside a period for doing an act` `test calendar: a day is a holiday` (YMD 2026 4 5) (YMD 2026 4 4) `ref before the period` (`Period of time` WITH `unit` IS Days, `how many` IS 1, `clear` IS FALSE)
